<div class="in"><h2>Luzon Volcanic Arc</h2><p>A chain of volcanoes running from Batanes and the Babuyan Islands south to Pinatubo, Taal, Mayon and Bulusan.</p><h2 style="font-size:20px;margin-top:14px">How it formed</h2><ul>
<li style="--i:0">The Eurasian (South China Sea) plate moves east and dives beneath Luzon along the Manila Trench.</li>
<li style="--i:1">About 100 km down, the sinking slab heats up and releases water into the hot mantle above it.</li>
<li style="--i:2">Water lowers the melting point of the rock, so magma forms, and being lighter, it rises.</li>
<li style="--i:3">Magma breaks through the crust and piles up as a curved line of volcanoes. In Bicol, subduction at the Philippine Trench also feeds the arc.</li></ul>
<h2 style="font-size:20px">Its volcanicity</h2><p>Arc magma is andesitic to dacitic: thick, sticky and gas-rich. Gas can't escape easily, so eruptions are often explosive and build steep stratovolcanoes.</p><ul><li style="--i:4">Hazards: ashfall, pyroclastic flows, lahars and phreatic (steam) blasts.</li><li style="--i:5">Types of activity range from effusive lava (Mayon 2023) to cataclysmic (Pinatubo 1991).</li></ul></div>`)}

